      "content": "I see. So you're suggesting that the driving force for these indigenous groups was primarily survival and maintaining tradition, whereas European progress was driven by a more active desire for \"domination\" and building \"complex systems.\" \n\nIt almost sounds like you're drawing a distinction between a passive, reactive way of living versus an active, intellectual way of shaping the world. Could you elaborate on that? I'm trying to understand if you're implying that the \"intellectual\" drive for systemic change is a characteristic that's more naturally aligned with Western development, rather than a universal human trait.",
